\f1\fs22 Piotr Dunhilton ate a breakfast of steak tartare and threw a bottle of milk at his cat.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 \'93This is my breakfast, you mangy animal,\'94 he lectured intemperately as the cat scuttled away to lick her coat clean.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The meat he chewed in his mouth was no longer a comfort for the splenetic carnivorous beast of his power-hungry soul. It felt like an oily chewed sponge; he spat it back on his plate.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Get me the Commissioner of Police,\'94 he told his tablet.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Calling Very Hard Man
\f1\i0 , it responded. After a few seconds, a coffee cup and Gerry\'92s face appeared on its thumb-smeared screen.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Morning CEO,\'94 Gerry answered. Without his tablet tagging the caller, he might not have recognised Dunhilton; he had left his tablet on the kitchen bench, and its camera caught little more than his exaggerated flabby chin and prominent Adam\'92s apple.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The Scarlet Shower Surgeon. Tell me we\'92ve caught the real one this time.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry slurped coffee. It sounded milky; Dunhilton never trusted anyone who didn\'92t drink coffee as black as their heart. \'93The identity of the killer is a delicate matter, CEO. It might be instructive to bring in the new Director of Freedom.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Hook him in,\'94 Dunhilton told his tablet. \'93The new Director, not the retired one.\'94\

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Calling Heston Whats-His-Name
\f1\i0 , the tablet responded.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>After new Freedom joined the meeting and minimal greetings were performed, Gerry prompted him. \'93Why don\'92t you tell the CEO about the Scarlet Shower Surgeon\'92s day job.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The new Director of Freedom noted the CEO\'92s familiarity with the Commissioner of TRAMPS, and was jealous. He swallowed hard.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Heston recognised the threat and decided to get ahead of it. \'93He\'92s one of ours, a deep-cover PAPER agent.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93One of yours, Heston?\'94 Dunhilton wheezed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Heston noted the CEO\'92s familiarity with him, and was terrified. He swallowed even harder. \'93Gone rogue, obviously,\'94 he said and almost choked on his words.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>If Heston was not already feeling enough heat, Gerry added, \'93A shame about the previous director, hugging a pillow like he did.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Heston gulped. His predecessor had 
\f2\i hugged a pillow
\f1\i0 \'97Dunhilton\'92s favourite disposal method for out-of-favour lackeys, throwing them out a window 
\f2\i just
\f1\i0  high enough the layer. The lower the window, the slower the fall through the Layer\'92s caustic haze before whatever remained hit Cloudborne ground.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton scrolled through reports looking for a profile on the killer. Nothing. Not even a name or a picture. \'93Who was this guy?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93His cover name was Haloren Smythe, your Serene Corporateness,\'94 Heston offered, hoping any information would mollify the CEO. \'93There\'92s quite a long chain of black ops involved here. CEO, it\'92s taking some time to unpick. I am yet to be read in on any of this, and all of it stems from long before my appointment as Director.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93This\'85 Haloren Smythe struck again on your watch, Heston,\'94 Dunhilton said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What do you know of Smythe\'92s assignments?\'94 Gerry prompted Heston, already knowing the answer.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93An operation has been running for\'85 well, many decades\'85 a white collar forensics op searching for hidden assets of long-dead billionaires. Their mission has two prongs. One is: recover assets and claim them for the great Airborne Meritocracy of Ulyssia; the other: find any potential claimants for the assets and neutralise them.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Which side of the operation was Smythe on?\'94 Dunhilton asked, expecting Smythe would be one of those \'93neutralising\'94 potential inheritors.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93He was on the financial forensic side of the operation. His code name was Midas, apparently because\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Everything he touched\'85\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yes, your Serene Corporateness.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry injected, \'93He was in the neighbourhood of the Stickett Inn when it was bombed,\'94 and allowed the corner of his mouth to curl with a smile. He watched Heston wilt.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton tried to hide his glee. \'93We caught the Scarlet Shower Surgeon and the bomber?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Is that right, Freedom?\'94 Gerry slurped his coffee.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Heston felt caught in a trap. He stammered.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Well, Heston?\'94 Dunhilton prompted.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Smythe struck the night of the bombing, in the vicinity of the Stickett Inn.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Well, I suppose that is some swift justice,\'94 Dunhilton remarked. \'93Who was the bomber?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Her name was Avi Grubler,\'94 Gerry answered for Heston, who squirmed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton zeroed in on Heston\'92s discomfort. \'93What?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Heston reluctantly said, \'93She was also one of ours.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>That took Dunhilton some moments to process. \'93You mean a civil servant?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Like a rodent watches a cat, Heston watched Gerry and, when it seemed Gerry might answer on his behalf, he admitted, \'93A PAPER agent.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Even via tablet conference, the other two might have heard Dunhilton\'92s gears seizing. His impatience escalated. Of all the things\'97the Elvi gatecrashing a holy joint venture service and the reception after\'97Dunhilton expected the Scarlet Shower Surgeon case would be the easiest to square away\'85\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'85 it would have been cleaner if Smythe had succeeded in murdering Sarah Jacobson before that idiot Alexander Shakti had caught him in the act. \'93You mean a PAPER agent murdered a PAPER agent? Why?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Heston chewed his words for a few seconds before he spat them out. \'93Smythe and Grubler were working on the same operation\'97Smythe on finding the assets, Grubler on neutralising potential heirs.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry took a different tack. \'93Grubler blew up a bar to assassinate a potential heir; that seems rather excessive.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton shrugged. \'93It was a Cloudborne bar. The thing I find incredible is, why would an Airborne travel under the Layer in the first place?\'94 That Grubler\'92s target might be Cloudborne occurred to none of them.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93CEO,\'94 Gerry said delicately. \'93There\'92s another wrinkle regarding Smythe.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton huffed. \'93Spit it out, Commissioner.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Neither I nor, dare I say, Heston can say for certain that Smythe is dead.\'94 Gerry waited for Dunhilton\'92s bluster; none blew. Gerry cleared his throat. \'93His body is missing.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93How the tax do you lose a body?\'94 Dunhilton demanded.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Heston permitted himself the brief luxury of feeling smug.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93There is no record of the ambulance ever delivering Haloren Smythe to any hospital or morgue,\'94 Gerry said. \'93It disappeared off the grid for a number of hours, then returned to the station, without Smythe\'92s body.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Then surely you\'92ve interviewed the drivers.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93They\'92ve also disappeared. Their contact details are fake, dead tablet numbers, home addresses are vacant lots or burned-down MFC joints.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton nodded. Memphis Fried Chicken joints were always burning down\'85 something to do with the colocation of deep friers, electricity and teenage hair products. But at least MFC fires were an effective cover for disappearing awkward political opponents. Dunhilton preferred coating them in hair gel then deep frying them. The fires often lit themselves.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Your Serene Corporateness?\'94 Gerry brought Dunhilton back from his reverie.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We have nothing on the ambulance drivers?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Well, there is one line we\'92re pursuing, CEO. Their names were given as Dick van Dyke and Rick von Reich. Those names are known to police. They\'92re frequently used as part-time PAPER grunts.\'94
